<!-- Section 1 -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — Data vs Model Scaling</div>
<h2>The Asymptotic Scaling Bottleneck</h2>
<p>
  Neural network scaling is celebrated for its predictable, monotonic improvement in loss. When we train models compute-optimally, we balance parameter counts and training steps to minimize loss. However, this raises a fundamental question: <strong>is a smaller model simply less sample efficient, or does it suffer from a more structural limitation?</strong>
</p>
<p>
  If we scale the dataset size to infinity ($D \to \infty$), can a smaller model with $N_s$ parameters eventually match the loss achieved by a larger model $N_l$ trained under compute constraints? 
</p>
<p>
  The paper develops a phenomenological scaling argument showing that the answer is **no**. Let's contrast two regimes:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Learnable via data scaling:</strong> The loss gap can be closed. The smaller model is merely under-trained and can catch up to the larger model by seeing more tokens.</li>
  <li><strong>Requires model scaling:</strong> A structural bottleneck. Even with infinite data ($L_\infty$), the smaller model's asymptotic loss remains strictly worse than the larger model's resource-constrained loss.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Asymptotic Loss Curve Comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Drag model size slider to see the asymptotic gap</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="loss-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="slider-group">
      <div class="slider-container">
        <label for="loss-size-slider">Small Model size ($N_s$):</label>
        <input type="range" id="loss-size-slider" min="10" max="60" value="25" />
      </div>
      <div class="stat-box">Asymptotic Gap: <span id="loss-gap-val">0.12</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The interactive visualization demonstrates this contrast. The shaded red region marks the loss that the smaller model is **mathematically locked out of**, even if trained on an infinite corpus. To explain that part of the data distribution, you must scale the model size.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2 -->
<section id="s-competition" data-code="capacity">
<div class="section-no">02 — Resource Competition</div>
<h2>Neuron Allocation & Feature Utilities</h2>
<p>
  Why does a smaller model fail to explain certain parts of the data distribution even under infinite data scaling? The root cause is <strong>data-induced competition over resources (neurons)</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  Consider a mixture of tasks $k \in \&#123;1, \dots, K\&#125;$ where each task appears with prior frequency $\pi_k$ and has feature eigenvalues $\lambda_&#123;k, j&#125;$. The paper models this with a shared multi-task encoder $U \in \mathbb&#123;R&#125;^&#123;d \times N&#125;$ and task-specific decoders. Under gradient descent, the encoder is driven to keep features in strict order of their **utilities**:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Feature Utility Formulation</div>
  <span class="math-var">u<sub>k, j</sub></span> = <span class="math-var">&pi;<sub>k</sub></span> &middot; <span class="math-var">&lambda;<sub>k, j</sub></span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment"># Utilities are determined by task frequency (&pi;<sub>k</sub>) and feature complexity (&lambda;<sub>k, j</sub>)</span>
</div>
<p>
  If a task is rare ($\pi_k$ is small) or complex (requires many directions, meaning $\lambda_&#123;k, j&#125;$ decays slowly), its feature utilities will be low. In a model with width $N$, the encoder only represents the top $N$ features with the largest utilities. 
</p>
<p>
  In a small model, all representation capacity is fully exhausted by the high-utility features of common tasks. Rare or complex tasks are completely starved out of the network representation.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Neuron Capacity Allocation Grid</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Toggle model size to see neuron specialization</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="neuron-grid-container">
      <div class="neuron-controls">
        <button class="neuron-btn active" id="btn-neuron-small">Small Model (16 Neurons)</button>
        <button class="neuron-btn" id="btn-neuron-large">Large Model (64 Neurons)</button>
      </div>
      <div class="neuron-layout">
        <div class="neuron-grid" id="neuron-grid"></div>
        <div class="neuron-legend">
          <div class="legend-item">
            <div class="legend-color c-common"></div>
            <span>Common Task Features</span>
          </div>
          <div class="legend-item">
            <div class="legend-color c-rare"></div>
            <span>Rare Task Features</span>
          </div>
          <div class="legend-item">
            <div class="legend-color c-empty"></div>
            <span>Unallocated / Free Capacity</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  As visualized above, a **Small Model** allocates all its scarce neurons to represent the common features. Scaling the model parameters to a **Large Model** does not merely improve accuracy on existing tasks; it frees up resources for common tasks, leaving spare capacity that allows the network to learn low-frequency, complex features.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3 -->
<section id="s-forgetting" data-code="retention">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Update-and-Forget Loop</div>
<h2>Catastrophic Update-and-Forget Dynamics</h2>
<p>
  The challenge of learning rare tasks is not just a static capacity problem—it is heavily shaped by learning dynamics. When a rare task is observed, gradient updates adjust the weights to capture it. But what happens in the steps in-between rare task observations?
</p>
<p>
  If the gap $G$ (steps between rare observations) is long, the gradient updates from frequent tasks dominate. In a narrow model, these frequent updates overwrite the rare task features, causing the model to forget them. The model gets trapped in an **update-and-forget loop**:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Rare task batch arrives:</strong> The representation alignment spikes.</li>
  <li><strong>Frequent task steps continue:</strong> The alignment decays exponentially back to near-zero.</li>
  <li><strong>Result:</strong> Zero cumulative learning.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Matched-Frequency Injection Simulation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Play simulation to observe rare feature retention over steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="retention-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="retention-controls">
      <button class="neuron-btn active" id="btn-ret-play">Play</button>
      <button class="neuron-btn" id="btn-ret-reset">Reset</button>
      <div style="margin-left:auto; display:flex; gap:1rem; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap">
        <div class="slider-container" style="min-width: 130px;">
          <label for="sel-ret-width">Width:</label>
          <select id="sel-ret-width" class="neuron-btn" style="background:var(--card2)">
            <option value="narrow">Narrow (Small)</option>
            <option value="wide" selected>Wide (Large)</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="slider-container" style="min-width: 130px;">
          <label for="sel-ret-gap">Gap G:</label>
          <select id="sel-ret-gap" class="neuron-btn" style="background:var(--card2)">
