<p>
  In the simulation, observe how a **Wide Model** maintains a baseline alignment even during long gaps $G$. The rare task signal decays slightly, but enough remains to build upon during the next injection step. In contrast, in a **Narrow Model**, the alignment decays completely to 0, resetting the learning progress to zero.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4 -->
<section id="s-gradients" data-code="gradients">
<div class="section-no">04 — Gradient Interference</div>
<h2>Gradient Weakening & Stability</h2>
<p>
  Why do larger models escape this overwriting loop? The paper formalizes this through a bound on the gradients. Let $F$ denote the set of frequent tasks, and $MF = \sum_&#123;k \in F&#125; \pi_k C_k$ denote their weighted covariance. The aggregate frequent-task gradient $GF(U)$ obeys:
</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 4: Gradient Bound</div>
  &parallel;<span class="math-var">G<sub>F</sub>(U)</span>&parallel;<sub>F</sub> &le; 2 &middot; &radic;<span class="math-var">&lambda;<sub>1</sub>(M<sub>F</sub>)</span> &middot; &radic;<span class="math-var">&delta;<sub>F</sub>(U)</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment"># Where &delta;<sub>F</sub>(U) = Tr((I - P<sub>U</sub>) M<sub>F</sub>) is the residual unexplained signal.</span>
</div>
<p>
  This bound has a profound implication: <strong>as the common tasks are explained, their residual error $\delta_F(U)$ goes to zero, causing their gradient updates to weaken (shrink in norm)</strong>.
</p>
<p>
  When a model is large enough ($N \ge N_F(\varepsilon)$), it has the capacity to explain the common tasks completely. Once solved, the frequent-task gradients weaken, leaving the parameters free for rare-task gradients to update the model without interference.
</p>
<p>
  Conversely, in smaller models, the common tasks are never fully solved due to capacity limits. Their residual error remains high, keeping their gradient updates large, which continuously overwrites the rare task.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">Gradient Interaction Vector Sandbox</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Adjust residual to observe how frequent gradient changes</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="gradient-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="slider-group">
      <div class="slider-container">
        <label for="grad-res-slider">Frequent Task Residual:</label>
        <input type="range" id="grad-res-slider" min="0" max="100" value="80" />
      </div>
      <div class="stat-box">Frequent Grad Norm: <span id="grad-norm-val">0.80</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Use the slider to simulate the learning process. As the **Frequent Task Residual** approaches 0 (signifying it has been learned), the frequent gradient vector $G_F$ (Purple) shrinks to zero. This allows the rare task gradient $G_R$ (Green) to dominate the update direction (White) without being overwritten.
</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5 -->
<section id="s-olmo" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">05 — Verification in OLMo</div>
<h2>Empirical Pretraining Sweeps</h2>
<p>
  To validate these theoretical claims, the authors pretrained OLMo models ranging from **4M to 4B parameters** on the Dolma v1.7 corpus (up to 210B tokens). They injected custom algorithmic tasks at controlled frequencies ($f \in [7.8 \times 10^&#123;-3&#125;, 2.4 \times 10^&#123;-8&#125;]$):
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Comparison (TCMP):</strong> Given prompt, predict if TOK1 &lt; TOK2 (requires learning global token order).</li>
  <li><strong>Modular Addition (TADD):</strong> Predict (TOK1 + TOK2) mod 100 (requires learning Fourier modes).</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The results strongly match the synthetic theory. At low frequencies, smaller models (4M, 20M) show random-chance performance (10% on modular addition, 50% on comparison), while larger models (300M, 1B, 4B) successfully learn the tasks.
</p>

